Subject: [PATCH net-next v1 00/12] net: flow_dissector: opt-in fast-path framework for common + encapsulated shapes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__skb_flow_dissect() parses packet headers with a generic protocol-graph
parser. This series adds an opt-in, static-branch-gated, straight-line
fast-path for the packet shapes that dominate real deployments. Each
shape has its own gate under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/, every gate is
disabled by default, and when a gate is off the added cost is one
not-taken branch per dissect.

Performance summary (wins first)
--------------------------------
Two kinds of win, measured across 3 ISAs and 8 microarchitectures, with
N=3 replicates in-kernel and per-shape byte-identical verification.

(a) Byte-identical shapes — reduction in dissector CPU cost (isolated
    per-shape microbench, all fast-paths compiled in; range across the 7
    measurable uarches):

      eth_ip    -4.7% .. -31.6%   (isolated eth+IPv4+TCP: -47% .. -55%)
      vlan     -18.1% .. -38.5%
      qinq     -16.1% .. -39.8%
      pppoe     -8.6% .. -28.0%
      mpls      -6.7% .. -15.0%
      ipip     -36.8% .. -47.0%   (largest; slow path re-parses inner IP)
      gre       byte-identical descent family (~ipip)

    In-kernel, cleanly above the N=3 noise floor: eth_ip -112 cyc/pkt
    (-4.9%) and vlan -148 cyc/pkt (-4.9%) on 25 GbE pairs. Consistent
    across architectures; largest on in-order cores.

(b) Encapsulation descent (vxlan_inner / geneve_inner / gtpu_inner) —
    CPU is ~net-zero; the win is flow distribution/identity for overlay
    traffic, on the real flow_hash_from_keys():

      today the outer tunnel collapses every inner flow onto ONE hash
      bucket (up to 512x hot-bucket imbalance — one queue/path doing all
      the work; GTP-U and fixed-source-port tunnels at any scale).
      Descent restores full per-inner-flow spread (~1.4x imbalance).
      Holds for IPv4 and IPv6 inner flows and underlays.

Detail, methodology, and honest caveats (including where kernel-sourced
VXLAN already spreads) follow.

It is presented as one framework, up front, rather than shape-by-shape,
so the intent is clear: land a byte-identical fast-path for the common
L2/L3/L4 shapes, and — the part datacenter and telco operators have asked
for — give the dissector's consumers the *inner* flow identity for the
overlay encapsulations (VXLAN, Geneve, GTP-U) they run at scale.

The dissector's output is not a local optimisation detail. flow_keys ->
skb->hash is the canonical flow identity consumed by ~50 call sites:
ECMP/multipath route selection, RSS, RPS/RFS, bonding/LAG
(xmit_hash_policy), tc-flower, and hardware aRFS. Everything that spreads
or classifies a flow derives from it.

The operator problem
--------------------
For VXLAN / Geneve / GTP-U traffic the dissector stops at the outer
tunnel header today, so every one of those consumers sees the *outer*
tunnel identity, not the inner flow. We measured the effect directly on
the real flow_hash_from_keys() (userspace port of the mainline dissector),
feeding a single tunnel-endpoint pair carrying 2000 distinct inner flows,
20000 packets. Numbers are the count of distinct hashes (flow-identity
resolution) and the 256-bucket occupancy (an RSS indirection table):

  GTP-U (dport 2152)           outer:    1 flow,   1/256 buckets, entropy 0.00
                               inner: 2000 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.99
  VXLAN/Geneve, fixed sport    outer:    1 flow,   1/256 buckets, entropy 0.00
                               inner: 2000 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.99

All 2000 inner flows collapse onto a single hash bucket. Every hash-based
consumer — an RSS queue, an RPS CPU, an ECMP nexthop, a LAG member — sees
one flow and can neither spread nor tell them apart. Descending into the
inner packet restores full resolution. GTP-U is the sharpest case: it has
no outer source-port entropy at all, and it is the mobile-core data path
where millions of subscriber flows ride one tunnel between two endpoints.

Being honest about VXLAN/Geneve: modern Linux derives the outer UDP
source port from the inner flow hash, so with a Linux sender the outer
tuple already carries per-inner-flow entropy. In that case:

  VXLAN/Geneve, kernel sport   outer: 1883 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.98
                               inner: 2000 flows, 256/256 buckets, entropy 0.99

For pure hash *spreading* (RSS/ECMP/bonding) the outer tuple is already
fine here — we are not claiming a spreading win for kernel-sourced VXLAN.
Descent's value in that case is *identity*: the outer source port spreads
but is opaque; a consumer that must read the actual inner flow —
tc-flower matching an inner 5-tuple for policy/offload, conntrack,
per-tenant accounting, ECMP path stability — cannot use it. The inner
5-tuple is invisible behind the tunnel regardless of source-port entropy.

The solution
------------
An opt-in inner descent (net.flow_dissector.{vxlan,geneve,gtpu}_inner),
off by default. When enabled it sets FLOW_DIS_ENCAPSULATION and dispatches
the inner Eth/IP/L4 (VXLAN/Geneve TEB) or inner IP (GTP-U) into the same
fast-path, so every consumer gets the correct inner identity at once. This
is a behaviour change (see "Contract" below), which is why the three
descent patches are marked RFC EXPERIMENT and posted for design discussion,
on top of the byte-identical foundation.

CPU results (byte-identical shapes)
-----------------------------------
Dissector cost in isolation, userspace A/B (same source compiled two
ways), eth + IPv4 + TCP, nanoseconds per packet, lower is better:

    x86 Zen 2  (Threadripper PRO 3945WX)   12.4 -> 6.6   -47%
    x86 Skylake (Core i9-10885H)           10.6 -> 5.6   -47%
    x86 Haswell (Celeron 2955U)            35.9 -> 18.0  -50%
    ARM Cortex-A53                        119.0 -> 61.3  -48%
    ARM Cortex-A72                         42.4 -> 19.1  -55%
    ARM Cortex-A76                         19.4 -> 9.3   -52%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60                    98.2 -> 49.3  -50%

The fast-path executes about half as many instructions per dissect,
consistent across three ISAs / eight microarchitectures; the relative
reduction is largest on in-order cores. IPv6 uses the same gate and is
byte-identical (x86 -39%, RISC-V -25%). One exception: on x86 Zen 1 the
base eth_ip reduction is not measurable at the median, though Zen 1 still
wins clearly on the more complex shapes.

Per-shape, with all fast-paths compiled in (the kernel-representative
case), percent reduction in dissector ns/pkt across the seven measurable
uarches:

    IP-in-IP    -37% .. -47%   (largest: the slow path re-runs the full
                                parser for the inner IP)
    VLAN / QinQ -16% .. -40%
    PPPoE        -9% .. -28%
    MPLS         -7% .. -15%

In-kernel, under kernel-pktgen small-packet load, the fast-path saving
appears as cycles/packet in the ksoftirqd perf-stat trace. Cleanly above
the N=3 run-to-run noise floor (2x pooled stddev): -148 cyc/pkt (-4.9%)
on an older mlx5 25 GbE pair (VLAN UDP), and -112 cyc/pkt (-4.9%) on a
Zen 2 25 GbE pair (eth_ip). The encapsulation descent is ~net-zero on CPU
(the inner work cancels the outer saving) — its benefit is the flow
identity above, not cycles. We state that plainly.

Safety and the byte-identical contract
--------------------------------------
Patches 1-7 are byte-identical: the fast-path either writes the same
flow_keys as the slow path, or it returns false and the slow path runs.
These are the only two outcomes, by construction; output was verified to
match the slow path per shape. They are proposed for merge.

Patch 8 (per-shape counters) does not change dissection at all — it adds
per-cpu counters and a read-only /proc/net/flow_dissector_stats. It is the
observability surface (see "Observability & adaptive enablement" below) and
is also proposed for merge.

Patches 9-11 (VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U inner) are a behaviour change: the slow
path stops at the outer tunnel today, so descending makes fast-path output
differ from slow-path output. Patch 12 (adaptive auto-enable) is an RFC that
adds a policy loop, not a new dissection path. Open questions for the list:

  - Should the slow path grow the same descent, so the contract stays
    "fast == slow" and the behaviour is available without the fast-path?
  - Per-netns config vs the global static_branch used here.
  - Tunnel port discovery: VXLAN is hardcoded to 4789; Cilium uses 8472,
    so production needs to consult the tunnel netdev. GTP-U handles only
    v1 G-PDU with no optional header.

Testing
-------
This series has been tested across three ISAs and eight microarchitectures
(x86 Zen1/Zen2/Skylake/Haswell, ARM Cortex-A53/A72/A76, RISC-V X60), with
N=3 replicates on the in-kernel runs and byte-identical verification per
shape. Methods:

  - userspace A/B dissector microbench (isolated ns/pkt), per shape;
  - kernel-pktgen cycles/pkt via ksoftirqd perf-stat (the clean CPU signal);
  - iperf3 wire-saturated macro (receiver softirq % under RSS/RPS/RFS and
    tc-flower load);
  - the encapsulation flow-hash distribution study above (real
    flow_hash_from_keys, outer vs inner);
  - 10-hour soaks; mixed real-traffic tcpreplay; PCAP miss-cost microbench
    (worst-case dispatcher fall-through); a flow_keys consumer/field audit;
    a 25 GbE line-rate characterisation; cross-uarch macro; a RISC-V
    dual-sender saturation stress; and a live WireGuard+bpftrace trace
    (__skb_flow_dissect ran 1.1M times in 10s on a single 902 Mbit/s flow).

Where the dissector runs
------------------------
The consumers, in rough order of relevance to the deployments this
targets: ECMP/multipath route selection, RSS, RPS/RFS, bonding/LAG,
tc-flower, hardware aRFS (mlx5/ice/bnxt/...), then the qdiscs
(fq/fq_codel/cake) and the rest. The improvement is a property of the hash
and keys, so it applies to all of them at once; no single consumer is the
mechanism.

Enabling
--------
Every gate defaults off; an unused gate is one not-taken branch. Enable the
shapes present in your traffic: the byte-identical gates (eth_ip, vlan, qinq,
pppoe, mpls, ipip, gre) are safe to turn on wherever that shape appears; the
descent gates (vxlan/geneve/gtpu_inner) change hashing behaviour and are
enabled deliberately for overlay (k8s) and telco (GTP-U) deployments. Each
sysctl is documented in Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst; a
per-feature "when to enable, by traffic pattern" guide (all ten shapes, with
caveats) accompanies the supporting data.

Observability & adaptive enablement
-----------------------------------
Ten opt-in gates raise two operator questions: "how do I see this working?"
and "how do I avoid hand-tuning ten knobs?" Both are answered by counters the
operator wants anyway.

Patch 8 adds per-cpu per-shape counters and a read-only
/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats: for each byte-identical shape, how many
packets the slow path handled (occurrences), how many the fast body handled
(fast_hits), and the resulting eligible fraction, plus the current gate state.
Because the fast path returns before the slow-path classification, exactly one
of the two is incremented per shaped packet depending on gate state, so
(occurrences + fast_hits) / dissects is a gate-invariant measure of how much
traffic each shape's fast body *would* handle — the signal for whether a gate
is worth enabling. Cost is one this_cpu_inc on the already-hot path, summed
only on read.

That signal makes enablement decidable rather than guessed, because we
measured *both* halves of the per-packet cost model: the per-match saving S
(per shape per uarch) and the hot-path miss cost C (the penalty a non-matching
packet pays when a gate is on). Enabling a shape is net-positive exactly when
its matching fraction exceeds the break-even p_be = C / (S + C) — a derived,
measured number per shape per uarch, not a heuristic (2-12% for the mainstream
byte-identical shapes on out-of-order cores, 8-19% on in-order; MPLS is the
high-bar exception).

Patch 12 (RFC) turns that into an in-kernel "auto" mode so the operator sets
one knob instead of ten. Decisions are driven by a packet-count window
(net.flow_dissector.auto_window_packets), not a wall-clock tick: every N
dissects the kernel compares each byte-identical shape's windowed eligible
fraction to its measured break-even and flips the gate, with hysteresis, a
dwell of several windows, and a flip rate cap. This is traffic-proportional
(a busy link adapts in milliseconds, an idle link does not churn) and adaptation
speed is one intuitive knob. The hot path only samples; the static_branch flips
happen in a workqueue. MPLS is not auto-managed (its saving barely exceeds the
miss cost on in-order cores) and the descent shapes are never auto-managed
(they change hashing — that must stay a deliberate operator action).

The same policy is also shipped as a userspace reference agent (reads the
stats file, writes the sysctls, identical thresholds), so the RFC presents
both homes — in-kernel loop vs "mechanism not policy" userspace agent — and
lets the list decide where the loop belongs. The counters (patch 8) are the
common, uncontroversial foundation for either.

Limitations
-----------
  - This is a per-packet CPU reduction (byte-identical shapes) and a
    per-packet identity fix (descent). On a host limited by link rate,
    packet rate, or queue depth it appears as available CPU / better
    spread, not as higher throughput. A 25 GbE characterisation confirmed
    the MTU-1500 ceiling on that hardware is a NIC packet-rate wall, not
    CPU.
  - Absolute nanosecond values are per-architecture baselines; the
    cross-architecture ratios are the comparison.
  - The kernel-sourced-VXLAN case already spreads (stated above); we do
    not overclaim it.

